#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "371d99ed-a060-51da-8f0f-7b38a0267bb7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a Decoupled System With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "a38dad3a-1afd-5e97-9046-87e413574865" :question/math-academy-id "e-21642" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-8y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/73/737c96109e65d59f4df2c2fa574737fe604483fe4ccc9772a3d2bf22ee32e55a.svg)\nwhere ![](images/22/22dbda7d3b169206071e5af49fd1fe1af28d86ef1e0a8b37d58eacb62b44c1d2.svg)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-8$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ![](images/5b/5bd691bbaa9542184d90e55aeb16f4dfcd3d22d092b4652e8bddba7bf0b926aa.svg)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/8b/8b25789fb45234e4299e4b5ce3371c1afafc181c64f9802d212ae7cbefc0970f.svg)\n\nNotice that for each vector ![](images/67/674212f0d815e0588faaf276bd797e9822ac5659e5d4b15a7635d7f1327d5dc5.svg) we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/d4/d4c31285e6f31a229bc6fc5cbe2d78e2bf50f73bb6305d91687c5dcc31c3885a.svg)\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector ![](images/26/268764bbe776b03647daac7fc77e8ed24107e1bb0cf16d955f7a845a12c65755.svg)\n\nSince $e^{-8t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty,$ so the origin is a stable node.\n\nThe phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/4c/4c337f7f6b80f340859844e8217525db98230660d22e760c70218f0c282d21e9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2c335ee-467a-5114-a7d9-4c2692562d9e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "e3e083fd-2421-5f66-acb5-69e1523e7fb0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21643" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $\\mathbf{v} = [1, \\ -1]^T$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [0, \\ 1]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ Sketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](images/72/72cfb513575d6481a4ee3206ff4d74568dc8397e10fec34ae89242a444a6b2d2.svg) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/46/461a22d18752e55eada19b3f0bb58be61bee7a9cd88437304f291c4a9992bdae.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/ed/ed2fb464c555b2cbf0ffa7f67084e5565fc7c9e80e0263647e1303f69a91895d.svg)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{-2t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/52/523dccf73169d02b6bdf4297e96ec84bcc6c43213cdd722000f3a987a534b218.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n![](images/05/0589d1749aed66b91a94b49e4a8815bc4a8621eef3b45e535d2117e81d4f416d.svg)\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/6c/6c84cd04f3b037bee48d02d3183114e2668fbbd110bd44e9311e7ff454db8bac.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166547 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "4aa5583f-003b-55a7-bfac-ae42ac44a380" :question/math-academy-id "e-21645" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of multiplicity two. Determine the type and stability of the equilibrium point for this linear system." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n![](images/dd/dda3aceadd2c3bc3b53422d9533f3b5926906de522137d2e0edc4794d658b1fd.svg)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a unstable node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a degenerate node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/32/32f9fd5ff34d75a7b82427a5ab7233613da3bdbaefdaef37058e65b1691b6c4a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "371d99ed-a060-51da-8f0f-7b38a0267bb7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a Decoupled System With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83780e2d-f983-56a0-910d-4315acbd104a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2a/2acd3b0de16138b460a332cf6e4841e6caeeb5dbb23eef8f411dbdb5ce48ea42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164486} {:answer/id #uuid "85c55197-972e-5ae8-adc5-35de8dcbd9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e48266d30ae7c204b25e6eddca968d1b4ce9cd8e1c7b19cd20247fa66070f0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164487} {:answer/id #uuid "aed621ee-dc79-51f3-b217-0d285b403ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ea/ea7ff4f032bade692953813c58b34ba62ab9c525c71ac13a2256dda6285e6620.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164488} {:answer/id #uuid "b77e3e0a-5eb0-5077-bf3d-98a59c2ef736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7be65c63a4e5dcbe73fbc63faf3f061476ecd478cef8d58776bb843bfe84df2c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164489} {:answer/id #uuid "6b10b504-d34f-55f9-9884-1fbd9169b090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83780e2d-f983-56a0-910d-4315acbd104a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2a/2acd3b0de16138b460a332cf6e4841e6caeeb5dbb23eef8f411dbdb5ce48ea42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164486} :answer-field/id #uuid "be5300bb-a569-52b1-b06c-9159f68b1f49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2b2a037-2ba8-540a-a005-ca472e5f4072" :question/math-academy-id "q-333267" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=5$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/2a/2acd3b0de16138b460a332cf6e4841e6caeeb5dbb23eef8f411dbdb5ce48ea42.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "371d99ed-a060-51da-8f0f-7b38a0267bb7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a Decoupled System With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05091666-67ba-551c-b9f1-c3ee8b2d8246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/87/87dfef6eaac680d1730a6b5537ecab4e5d1abee35dd58abf80c72c3afa01107b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305197} {:answer/id #uuid "35a31eb6-922e-5f1c-aa3e-46856846b11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/904ec8bfe5c8f1b9a63becd782c82da68ef2a70894368418c3710d09bc029db1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305665} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a96595-9e8d-5646-bfe7-092058fb51f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6b/6bdaece2579041f23a73323c4317751dfd8266364633bd2af258437b53d4a6f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306865} {:answer/id #uuid "c30f08fa-f2db-5f11-8d03-086b6d17e6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e48266d30ae7c204b25e6eddca968d1b4ce9cd8e1c7b19cd20247fa66070f0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306969} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4cd576-4ddf-5584-ab7a-a1ab48e29122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35a31eb6-922e-5f1c-aa3e-46856846b11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/904ec8bfe5c8f1b9a63becd782c82da68ef2a70894368418c3710d09bc029db1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305665} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e02809-ee75-5c7f-a812-9bb5ee0dd647" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83d7fadd-8088-5133-921f-95c4d7994bd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-333270" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/90/904ec8bfe5c8f1b9a63becd782c82da68ef2a70894368418c3710d09bc029db1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "371d99ed-a060-51da-8f0f-7b38a0267bb7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a Decoupled System With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22875c88-e98a-556f-bd92-7742a4417f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/745db6e4166ed6af3b7a3c1532bb23d7d6e7506f75c4f371430bd5d90a1a1d02.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425276} {:answer/id #uuid "271019b1-15c6-5b35-bc59-a9d577948553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8ebbd0147ad94cd62d952796b3171dba98b1388544fe5f1b379f981425477063.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425279} {:answer/id #uuid "340a8062-814c-54fb-9be9-64f8d4dd51e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425281} {:answer/id #uuid "5c72915c-1617-5d86-9810-6ff702b7c1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/71/71b5e685e876b0e7e33a3db5e1edc3579730d179867fe3b17b05574942201181.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425289} {:answer/id #uuid "ba67a73d-dbf2-5baa-971e-eccb95c34b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e48266d30ae7c204b25e6eddca968d1b4ce9cd8e1c7b19cd20247fa66070f0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c72915c-1617-5d86-9810-6ff702b7c1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/71/71b5e685e876b0e7e33a3db5e1edc3579730d179867fe3b17b05574942201181.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425289} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed34ebf7-d0c0-5560-bc6c-feaad1d7a4a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9addc7f5-7ff7-5987-87ac-e05cc5e87102" :question/math-academy-id "q-333272" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/71/71b5e685e876b0e7e33a3db5e1edc3579730d179867fe3b17b05574942201181.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "371d99ed-a060-51da-8f0f-7b38a0267bb7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a Decoupled System With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252d0ad7-d5cd-50c4-be35-c39d84a2074f"