7592186425318} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf175a51-5f83-55f9-bec0-896057053ccd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2b9f8e2-b39f-52a6-8433-15b3bb05fe5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333485" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=2(t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.37$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $2$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/42/4232a3293f2414f6121c834fa09145f5f3b573d83ad7a3695c6ca93c228c8563.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e78fbd54f2825d28581b8181cab7762b7a625f7b21620eec37436771c9e1695.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =2,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ y=2(t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.37$ | $0$ | $0.60$ |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $2$ | $3.58$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/42/4232a3293f2414f6121c834fa09145f5f3b573d83ad7a3695c6ca93c228c8563.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e78fbd54f2825d28581b8181cab7762b7a625f7b21620eec37436771c9e1695.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1750bded-03a2-535d-9322-af370f69f5d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391221} {:answer/id #uuid "7b95026e-9297-5fe5-b515-53db91622691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b95026e-9297-5fe5-b515-53db91622691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391222} :answer-field/id #uuid "23101e77-153a-53ab-8eaa-fb91f151bfef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4552e55a-71fb-5355-a8ff-c44fe89b0c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391224} {:answer/id #uuid "79852ea3-d668-55cf-ae57-0e55042a72c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4552e55a-71fb-5355-a8ff-c44fe89b0c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391224} :answer-field/id #uuid "5874e815-f2f7-5127-b199-5dfa1e8dd28e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04b55cbe-b4be-543b-bbe4-7f5a1dc7e192" :question/math-academy-id "q-333614" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c1/c1d7f49dfe79bcd36394e4b0bb49180167ac7a8791555f0f6974f5fe49fa249f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b15abd56-8ff1-5940-9908-64440b2f7303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425302} {:answer/id #uuid "f89e8a56-5742-5f30-a939-70f76c6deb0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b15abd56-8ff1-5940-9908-64440b2f7303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425302} :answer-field/id #uuid "3007b7e8-b0aa-517c-b7ed-d47324656b6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425321} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dead613-6310-5d03-ae0a-f9d87e3caec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425273} {:answer/id #uuid "24e84d6a-bff5-5bb2-b795-aa9cf9834a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24e84d6a-bff5-5bb2-b795-aa9cf9834a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425278} :answer-field/id #uuid "f63bded0-5eca-5ef0-8469-313f1af281d1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc362b82-7598-58e4-b9cc-cf1f2a033f38" :question/math-academy-id "q-333667" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has only one eigenvalue. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =-2$ and it's negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/94/94f722ae71911d6b6967e55922301e771753078302aef950e1215d1fead75289.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "223862d4-0ce2-5e4a-ba6c-0e2e05a1a8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305478} {:answer/id #uuid "f474d111-156d-5bf5-b3cc-c97463a138bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f474d111-156d-5bf5-b3cc-c97463a138bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307413} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c7355ff-9284-5fde-81e0-bca9bc975af3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8996b55-40b3-5d46-a519-3a9f273ef6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307298} {:answer/id #uuid "ef13a079-43ab-5ec4-bf18-9df035d64273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef13a079-43ab-5ec4-bf18-9df035d64273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307345} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8bb0cc5-f12a-525d-a29f-a4a6e34ca991" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b699002a-13ee-52d0-9a5f-852554c5f030" :question/math-academy-id "q-333672" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =1$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there are infinitely many linearly independent eigenvectors. So, we have a $\\text{star}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/0e/0e2180fe05500bcff2d0a1e2c066e2364eea0fd481a63866578a07e8f46b3825.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems